---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';

export const lastUpdated = '2026-10-28';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: 'Cookie Policy',
  url: `${SITE.domain}/cookies/`,
  dateModified: lastUpdated,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: SITE.author },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.publisher },
};
---

<Base
  title="Cookie Policy — What This Site Stores"
  description="Which cookies and local storage DevSnap uses, what each one does, who sets it, and how to control or delete it."
  canonicalPath="/cookies/"
  schemas={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <ol class="crumbs">
      <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
      <li>Cookie Policy</li>
    </ol>

    <header class="tool-hero">
      <h1>Cookie Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The short version: the tools on this site set no cookies at all. The
        advertising that funds the site uses cookies, and this page says exactly
        which ones and how to opt out.
      </p>
      <p class="updated-note">Last updated {lastUpdated}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose mt-3">
      <h2>1. What this site stores in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        The developer tools on {SITE.name} run entirely in your browser and do
        not set any cookies. They use your browser's <strong>local storage</strong>
        for two things, and local storage is not a cookie: it is never sent to
        a server and does not travel with a request.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>devsnap-theme</code> — whether you chose the light or the dark interface. Used so the site does not flash the wrong colours on your next visit.</li>
        <li><code>devsnap-consent</code> — your answer to the advertising consent banner, so it is not shown again on every page.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Clearing your browser's site data removes both. Nothing else is stored,
        and no identifier of any kind is created for you by the tools.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Cookies set by the advertising</h2>
      <p>
        This site is funded by advertising, and the ad networks involved set
        their own cookies and similar technologies. They are set by Google
        AdSense and its partners, not by us, and they are used to serve
        advertising and to measure how effective it is.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Name</th>
              <th>Set by</th>
              <th>Purpose</th>
              <th>Type</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>IDE</code></td>
              <td>Google (DoubleClick)</td>
              <td>Builds a profile of interests based on your activity across sites, used to personalise advertising</td>
              <td>Third-party</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>test_cookie</code></td>
              <td>Google (DoubleClick)</td>
              <td>Checks that your browser accepts cookies, set when an ad is served</td>
              <td>First-party</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>_gcl_au</code></td>
              <td>Google AdSense</td>
              <td>Stores the conversion value when somebody clicks an ad</td>
              <td>First-party</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>ANID</code></td>
              <td>Google</td>
              <td>Distinguishes browsers and devices for advertising measurement</td>
              <td>Third-party</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>DSID</code>, <code>RUL</code></td>
              <td>Google</td>
              <td>Records which ads you have seen and which you have not</td>
              <td>First-party</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The full list of cookies Google may set is documented by Google itself,
        and the list changes over time. The table above covers the ones that
        apply to a site of this kind.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Consent</h2>
      <p>
        The first time you arrive, a banner asks whether you accept advertising
        cookies. Until you answer, the answer is treated as <strong>no</strong>,
        and that means the same thing everywhere: <strong>no advertising
        request is made at all</strong>. The ad slots are in the page, but they
        stay empty and nothing is sent to Google's advertising servers.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you accept, the advertising appears straight away, without the page
        being reloaded. If you reject, the banner does not come back and the site
        keeps working exactly as before. You can change your answer at any time
        using the "Cookie choices" link in the footer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google's advertising script is present in every page from the start,
        and Google's own consent mode is declared as denied before it loads.
        That is deliberate: the script has to be there for the site to be
        reviewable, and what waits for your answer is the ad request, not the
        script.
      </p>
      <p>
        Google requires a consent platform certified under its EU user consent
        policy for personalised advertising in those regions. When the site
        serves advertising, that platform (Google's own, Funding Choices) takes
        over the prompt: this site's banner waits for it to appear and then
        stands down, so you are never asked the same question twice.
      </p>
      <h2>4. How to control cookies</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Use the "Cookie choices" link in the footer to change your answer.</li>
        <li>Delete cookies in your browser settings. Every major browser can block or clear cookies for a single site.</li>
        <li>Opt out of personalised advertising from Google at <a href="https://adssettings.google.com/" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">adssettings.google.com</a>.</li>
        <li>Opt out of third-party vendor cookies at <a href="https://www.aboutads.info/choices/" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">aboutads.info</a>.</li>
        <li>Use <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/dnts" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google's Do Not Track</a> control, which is honoured by Google advertising.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Do Not Track</h2>
      <p>
        Some browsers send a Do Not Track header. The site honours it: when the
        header is present, <strong>no advertising request is made at all</strong>,
        even if you previously pressed Accept. You do not have to trust that
        sentence — the check is in the page source and it runs before any ad
        slot is requested.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Third-party embeds</h2>
      <p>
        The tools do not embed third-party content, so no other site receives
        information about your visit. Outbound links to other sites are
        ordinary links, and those sites have their own policies.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Changes and contact</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it. Questions:
        <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
